#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script --quiet
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["tyro"]
# ///
"""Control the Mullvad exit node: pick one, pin one, move off one, drop it, and keep it working.

Tailscale's automatic mode (auto:any) ranks exit nodes by latency and nothing else. Two failures stay invisible to it:

- Cloudflare scores each egress IP on its own and hard-blocks some Mullvad IPs for Chrome-family clients (every Electron app: Vesktop, ...), while a browser on the same IP passes. The app shows "Temporary Network Error" and Tailscale sees nothing wrong.
- A pinned node has no failover of its own: when it dies, all egress dies with it.

The watcher (`vpn watch`, the vpn-watch unit) is the one process that changes the exit node. `vpn on`, `to`, `next` and `off` send it a request and print what it streams back, and fail at once when it is not running. `vpn-off` (a bare `tailscale set --exit-node=`) turns the VPN off without it, and the watcher leaves what it sets alone.

A move, asked for or the watcher's own, sends a handshake probe to its next 8 nodes at once. It then tries the ones that answered in turn, printing each before it waits on it. It pins the first that carries traffic and passes the Discord check; the watcher's own moves run that check only while Vesktop runs. A node carries traffic when, within 5 s of being set, a TCP connect to 1.1.1.1 or 8.8.8.8 goes through and tailscaled counts bytes coming back from it. The Discord check asks Discord's API with a Chrome user agent. A site that blocks one node for its own reasons (YouTube does, for some Mullvad IPs) is not checked for: `vpn next` is the way off such a node. When no node answers or none passes, the exit node goes back to what it was before the command or the recovery, whether that was a pinned node, automatic mode or off; Ctrl-C at a command does the same.

Where a move goes from a node: the rest of its city, then the rest of its country, then Germany, Austria, Switzerland and the Netherlands, city by city, skipping nodes that report themselves offline. A city's nodes are dealt round-robin by provider series, the hundreds digit of a node's number, into one fixed cycle. The first node tried is the one after the current node in that cycle; each after it is the next one in the cycle from another provider, while the city has one. So repeated `vpn next` visits every node of a city before any twice, and a provider that fails costs one try, not the whole move.

When the watcher has to leave a node, it moves off it the same way, and after a move that pinned nothing it tries again 5 minutes later.

Every change of exit node, the watcher's own and every one made outside vpn, and every time the exit node went silent, is one line in ~/logs/runs/vpn.jsonl (bin/run-log). The watcher also writes an hourly heartbeat to ~/logs/runs/vpn-watch.jsonl.

Needs the tailscale operator grant (no sudo).

Examples:

    vpn status
    vpn list
    vpn list Germany
    vpn to Berlin
    vpn to de-fra-wg-202
    vpn next
    vpn on
    vpn off
"""

import errno
import http.client
